(4, 11, 'Residual volume is the amount of air:', '["Inhaled during a normal breath", "Exhaled forcefully after normal expiration", "Remaining in the lungs after maximal expiration", "Inhaled during maximal inspiration beyond tidal volume"]'::jsonb, 2),
(4, 12, 'The short period that occurs during the transmission of impulses through the synapses is:', '["Synaptic simulation", "Synaptic junction", "Synaptic cleft", "Synaptic delay"]'::jsonb, 3),
(4, 13, 'The heart''s four chambers consist of:', '["Two atria and two ventricles", "Four ventricles only", "Four atria only", "One atrium and three ventricles"]'::jsonb, 0),
(4, 14, 'The kidneys contribute to regulation of acid-base balance mainly by:', '["Producing insulin", "Excreting or reabsorbing hydrogen ions and bicarbonate", "Regulating heart rate", "Producing bile"]'::jsonb, 1),
(4, 15, 'Water reabsorbed by the collecting duct enters the:', '["Nephron loop", "Minor calyx", "Ureter", "Vasa recta"]'::jsonb, 3),
(4, 16, 'A nurse inadvertently infuses an IV solution containing potassium chloride too rapidly. Which of the following is an appropriate intervention advised by the physician?', '["Rapid infusion of IV Ringer\\''s solution", "Rapid infusion of IV NS 0.9%", "Insulin added to 10% dextrose in water solution", "Administration of albumin"]'::jsonb, 2),
(4, 17, 'Blood transfusion reactions can be classified broadly as:', '["Only immediate reactions exist; delayed reactions are not possible", "Immediate (e.g. acute haemolytic, allergic) and delayed (e.g. delayed haemolytic, iron overload) reactions", "Only allergic reactions occur", "Reactions cannot be classified in any meaningful way"]'::jsonb, 1),
(4, 18, 'Type 1 Respiratory Failure is characterised by:', '["Low PaO2 with normal or low PaCO2", "High PaCO2 only", "Normal PaO2 and PaCO2", "High PaO2 with low PaCO2"]'::jsonb, 0),
(4, 19, 'To prevent graft-versus-host disease, blood is:', '["Leukocyte-reduced", "Washed", "Irradiated", "Frozen"]'::jsonb, 2),
(4, 20, 'A Modified Allen''s Test that shows NO flushing after releasing the ulnar artery indicates:', '["Safe to proceed with radial puncture", "Inadequate collateral circulation — avoid that site", "The patient is dehydrated", "A normal, expected finding"]'::jsonb, 1),
(4, 21, 'The normal haemoglobin range for an adult male is approximately:', '["6–8 g/dL", "13.5–17.5 g/dL", "20–24 g/dL", "2–4 g/dL"]'::jsonb, 1),
(4, 22, 'George Kent is a 54-year-old widower with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and was rushed to the emergency department with increasing shortness of breath, pyrexia, and a productive cough with yellow-green sputum. He has difficulty communicating because of his inability to complete a sentence. One of his sons, Jacob, says he has been unwell for three days. Upon examination, crackles and wheezes can be heard in the lower lobes; he has tachycardia and a bounding pulse. Measurement of arterial blood gas shows pH 7.3, PaCO2 68 mm Hg, HCO3 28 mmol/L, and PaO2 60 mm Hg. How would you interpret this?', '["Metabolic Alkalosis, Uncompensated", "Metabolic Acidosis, Partially Compensated", "Respiratory Acidosis, Uncompensated", "Respiratory Acidosis, Partially Compensated"]'::jsonb, 3),
