(7, 104, 'Which inhalational anaesthetic agent has the lowest blood-gas partition coefficient among the following, resulting in the most rapid induction and emergence?', '["Halothane", "Isoflurane", "Desflurane", "Sevoflurane"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 105, 'Mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2) monitoring provides information about:', '["The balance between total body oxygen delivery and oxygen consumption", "Arterial carbon dioxide levels exclusively", "Airway resistance only", "Renal perfusion exclusively"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 106, 'Vasoconstrictors (e.g. adrenaline) added to local anaesthetics are contraindicated in:', '["Regional anaesthesia", "Spinal anaesthesia", "Epidural anaesthesia", "Digital nerve block"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 107, 'Decreased lung compliance (e.g., ARDS, pulmonary fibrosis) on a volume-controlled ventilator will manifest as:', '["Decreased peak airway pressure", "No change in airway pressures", "Increased exhaled tidal volume", "Increased peak and plateau airway pressures"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 108, 'A patient s/p abdominal surgery has a BP of 85/50 mmHg and HR 120 bpm. The nurse suspects:', '["Hemorrhage", "Pain", "Infection", "Atelectasis"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 109, 'A nurse is caring for a patient who is sedated. The nurse should ensure that the patient''s dignity is maintained by:', '["Leaving the patient''s door open", "Talking about the patient''s condition with other staff members in front of them", "Providing privacy and explaining all care procedures to the patient, even if they are unresponsive", "Only providing care when the patient''s family is present"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 110, 'Physical signs of the alarm stage of stress include the following EXCEPT:', '["Dilated pupils", "Tachycardia", "Hypoventilation", "Trembling"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 111, 'An advantage of inhalational over injectable anaesthetics is that:', '["Depth of anaesthesia is easier to control and adjust", "They never cause respiratory depression", "No equipment is needed", "They have no cardiovascular effects"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 112, 'Which physiological response is commonly associated with surgical stress?', '["Bronchial constriction", "Decreased cortisol levels", "Peripheral vasodilation", "Sodium and water retention"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 113, 'Decreasing level of consciousness in a severe asthmatic patient is classified as:', '["A relative indication for intubation", "An absolute indication for intubation", "Not related to asthma severity", "A sign of improvement"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 114, 'For small or patchy burns, a quick estimate of surface area can be made using:', '["The patient''s own palm ≈ 1% TBSA", "The Rule of Nines only", "Lund and Browder chart only in adults", "No estimation method exists"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 115, 'Which of the following recognized causes of DKA is most common?', '["Missed insulin treatment", "Newly diagnosed diabetic", "Underlying infection", "Stress"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 116, 'The following are manifestations of SIRS EXCEPT:', '["Temperature >38 °C", "Temperature <36 °C", "Heart rate >90 beats/min", "Systolic blood pressure >90 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 117, 'Wound debridement in tetanus management serves the purpose of:', '["Halting further toxin production at the source", "Providing pain relief only", "Improving cosmetic appearance", "Reducing the need for antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 118, 'Which of the following liver failure types occurs in a liver that was previously completely normal, over 2–12 weeks?', '["Fulminant", "Subacute/subfulminant", "Chronic (PSE)", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 119, 'All the followings are aseptic barriers except?', '["Sterile gloves and sterile gowns", "Sterile masks and sterile drapes", "Drapes and sterile gowns", "Antiseptic wipes"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 120, 'Which is NOT a sign of potential sepsis?', '["Tachycardia", "Tachypnoea", "Hypertension", "Raised white cell count"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 121, 'The ''Disability'' step of the ABCDE primary survey primarily assesses:', '["The patient''s insurance and financial status", "Neurological status, such as level of consciousness and pupillary response", "Only the presence of physical disabilities pre-existing before the injury", "Skin condition only"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 122, 'The steps of thyroid hormone synthesis begin with:', '["Trapping iodine", "Releasing TSH", "Forming T3/T4 directly from tyrosine without iodine", "Activating the pituitary gland"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 123, 'The best initial treatment for mild hypothermia is:', '["IV fluids", "Immersing the person in hot water", "Moving to a warm environment and providing dry clothing", "Giving caffeine"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 124, 'Which is NOT the type of shock?', '["Cardiogenic", "Hypovolemic", "Anaphylactic", "Aseptic"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 125, 'Supraglottic inhalational injury is primarily caused by:', '["Heat", "Chemical irritants only", "Carbon monoxide only", "Bacterial infection"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 126, 'ICU patients account for approximately what proportion of all hospital-acquired infections, despite occupying fewer than 10% of beds?', '[">5%", ">20%", ">50%", ">90%"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 127, 'Curling’s ulcer prevention in burn patients includes:', '["Proton pump inhibitors", "Antacids", "Sucralfate", "All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 128, 'A 50 years old male admitted in medical ward B at HMC was injected with an IV antibiotic that has caused severe irritation, pain at the site and respiratory distress generally. What can be the possible diagnosis?', '["Side effect", "skin allergy", "adverse reaction", "Local inflammatory reaction"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 129, 'Lactulose is used in the management of hepatic encephalopathy primarily to:', '["Increase serum ammonia levels", "Reduce serum ammonia by promoting its excretion in the stool", "Treat variceal bleeding directly", "Lower blood glucose"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 130, 'Management of medullary thyroid carcinoma differs from papillary/follicular types in that:', '["Radioiodine and radiotherapy are not helpful", "Radioiodine is always curative", "Surgery is never performed", "It requires no lymph node assessment"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 131, 'A nurse is performing initial emergency care for a burn patient. The first priority is to:', '["Administer pain medication", "Start fluid resuscitation", "Stop the burning process", "Cover the burned area with a dry sterile dressing"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 132, 'For a patient with uncontrolled hemorrhage, permissive hypotension is used to:', '["Prevent coagulopathy", "Avoid dislodging clots", "Reduce blood loss", "All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 133, 'H. pylori eradication is significant in peptic ulcer disease because it:', '["Has no effect on ulcer recurrence", "Provides a non-surgical cure for many peptic ulcers", "Is only relevant to gastric cancer, not ulcers", "Increases the risk of rebleeding"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 134, 'What class of drug typically used to treat neurogenic shock?', '["Beta blocker", "Calcium channel blocker", "Loop diuretics", "Vasopressor"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 135, 'Hypotension in pregnancy is defined as a blood pressure below:', '["90/60 mmHg", "100/70 mmHg", "110/80 mmHg", "120/90 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 136, 'Complications of hyperbilirubinemia include the followings except', '["Encephalopathy", "Cerebral palsy", "White teeth", "Hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 137, 'Secondary postpartum haemorrhage occurs within what timeframe after delivery?', '["First 24 hours only", "Day 1 up to 6 weeks postpartum", "Only after 3 months", "Only in the first hour"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 138, 'Which statement about hypertensive disorders of pregnancy is true?', '["Placental pathology is implicated in the aetiology of pre-eclampsia", "Gestational hypertension means any BP ≥140/90 at any time in pregnancy", "The Korotkoff V sound marks systolic pressure", "Prolonged barrier contraception is protective"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 139, 'Which of the following is monitored to detect magnesium sulphate toxicity?', '["Blood glucose", "Tendon reflexes, respiratory rate, urine output", "Liver enzymes only", "Body temperature"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 140, 'Following a shoulder dystocia delivery, the nurse should assess the newborn closely for signs of:', '["No specific concern beyond routine assessment", "Brachial plexus injury and clavicle fracture, recognised complications of shoulder dystocia", "Only skin bruising", "Congenital heart disease specifically"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 141, 'Which is true about vomiting in pregnancy?', '["Only a few cases resolve by the second trimester", "90% resolve only at about 32 weeks", "When another cause is found it is called hyperemesis", "It may start as early as 6 weeks'' gestation"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 142, 'A minimum respiratory rate of what value should be maintained during MgSO4 therapy?', '[">6/min", ">12/min", ">30/min", "No specific value is required"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 143, 'During an eclamptic seizure, bed rails should be:', '["Left completely open", "Padded to prevent injury", "Removed entirely", "Irrelevant to patient safety"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 144, 'The difference between placenta praevia and abruption is:', '["In praevia the placenta covers or lies near the cervix; in abruption a normally sited placenta separates from the uterine wall", "In praevia the placenta separates; in abruption it covers the cervix", "They are the same condition", "Neither involves bleeding"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 145, 'Malrotation with volvulus in an infant is a surgical emergency mainly because it can cause:', '["Mild, self-limiting abdominal discomfort", "Bowel ischaemia from twisting of the mesentery, risking bowel necrosis", "Only skin rash", "Chronic mild constipation"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 146, 'A child presenting with epiglottitis should generally be managed by:', '["Immediately examining the throat with a tongue depressor", "Avoiding upsetting/examining the throat unnecessarily and urgently involving anaesthesia/ENT for controlled airway management", "Sending the child home with oral antibiotics", "Encouraging the child to lie flat immediately"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 147, 'A neonate is diagnosed with respiratory distress syndrome and given exogenous surfactant. The rationale for this treatment is that surfactant:', '["Increases surface tension in the alveoli", "Reduces alveolar surface tension, preventing alveolar collapse and improving lung compliance", "Has no effect on lung mechanics", "Is only used to treat infection"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 148, 'A nurse is caring for a newborn with birth asphyxia. The nurse knows that the main cause of birth asphyxia is:', '["A genetic condition", "A lack of oxygen to the newborn''s brain", "A bacterial infection", "A birth injury"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 149, 'The typical age for epiglottitis in children is:', '["1–3 months", "2–5 years", "5–10 years", "10–15 years"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 150, 'Which of the following statements is false?', '["A neonate is an infant", "The neonatal period is the first week of life", "Neonatology is part of perinatology", "Mortality is high in the neonatal period"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 151, 'A client develops an autoimmune reaction that attacks her own joint tissue. Using Neuman''s classification, this stressor would be categorized as:', '["Extrapersonal", "Interpersonal", "Intrapersonal", "Suprapersonal"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 152, 'A nurse compares a client''s current status to data obtained several months ago during a scheduled follow-up visit. This type of assessment is called:', '["Focused assessment", "Emergency assessment", "Time-lapsed reassessment", "Initial assessment"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 153, 'Which of the following is NOT typically an objective of quality assurance?', '["Improving patient satisfaction", "Ensuring accountability of health professionals", "Increasing the number of untrained staff to cut costs", "Promoting effective use of resources"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 154, 'The Nursing Interventions Classification (NIC) is best described as:', '["A standardized classification of treatments and interventions that nurses perform based on clinical judgment and knowledge", "A classification of medical diagnoses", "A classification of patient outcomes", "A billing code set used only for insurance claims"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 155, 'Which of the following is a potential consequence of poor quality assurance in nursing practice?', '["Increased patient satisfaction", "Improved patient outcomes", "Medication errors and adverse events", "Decreased nurse workload"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 156, 'Which factor is LEAST likely to directly influence where an individual falls on the health-illness continuum at a given moment?', '["Genetic and physiological factors", "Sociocultural and environmental factors", "The individual''s coping behaviors and health practices", "The brand of the hospital bed the client is assigned to"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 157, 'Which best describes ''reflexivity'' in the context of reflective nursing practice?', '["An ongoing awareness of how one''s own values, biases, and emotions influence clinical decisions and interactions with patients", "An involuntary physical reflex response to a stimulus", "A scheduled quality assurance chart audit", "A category of NANDA nursing diagnosis"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 158, 'Which of these best differentiates internal nursing audit from external audit?', '["Internal audit is conducted by the hospital''s own staff/committee, while external audit is conducted by an outside body such as an accrediting agency", "They are identical processes", "External audit uses no standards", "Internal audit is only about finances"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 159, 'A nurse rapidly assesses an unresponsive trauma patient''s airway, breathing, and circulation. This is an example of what type of assessment?', '["Initial comprehensive assessment", "Focused assessment", "Emergency assessment", "Time-lapsed reassessment"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 160, 'A client has the following nursing diagnoses: "Impaired Gas Exchange," "Risk for Impaired Skin Integrity," and "Anxiety." Which should the nurse address as the HIGHEST priority?', '["Anxiety", "Risk for Impaired Skin Integrity", "Impaired Gas Exchange", "All three simultaneously with equal priority"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 161, 'Why is reporting near misses, not just actual harmful events, important in risk management?', '["Near misses provide valuable information for identifying system weaknesses before they cause actual harm", "Near misses are legally irrelevant and should be ignored", "Reporting near misses increases hospital liability with no benefit", "Near misses cannot be prevented, so reporting is pointless"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 162, 'Summative evaluation is best described as:', '["A continuous, real-time check during care", "A final review of the whole care plan against stated objectives", "Something done before any care is given", "Only used for formative goals"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 163, 'Which scale of measurement captures data in mutually exclusive categories with no order?', '["Ratio", "Interval", "Ordinal", "Nominal"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 164, 'Which of the following is a research (data-collection) tool?', '["Graph", "Diagram", "Illustration", "Questionnaire"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 165, 'The following are measuring scales used in research EXCEPT:', '["Nominal scale", "Ordinal scale", "Interval scale", "Weighing scale"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 166, 'A ''comparative design'' in research is used to', '["Examine differences between two or more naturally occurring groups on one or more variables", "Randomly assign subjects to groups and manipulate an intervention", "Explore lived experiences using unstructured interviews only", "Analyze historical archives exclusively"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 167, 'Body temperature measured in degrees Celsius is an example of which scale of measurement?', '["Nominal", "Ordinal", "Interval", "Ratio"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 168, 'The most effective interview style is:', '["Directive", "Non-directive", "Semi-structured only", "A combination of directive and non-directive"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 169, 'Risk/Benefit Ratio is a consideration under which ethical principle?', '["Beneficence", "Justice", "Confidentiality", "Plagiarism avoidance"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 170, 'The hardest section to write in research is?', '["Statement of problems", "Hypothesis", "Discussion of major findings", "Results"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 171, 'Which is NOT a type of validity?', '["Face validity", "Content validity", "Unpredictable validity", "Construct validity"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 172, 'Applied research in Critical Care Nursing is best described as research that', '["Uses findings to solve practical clinical problems and improve patient care directly", "Is conducted only in university laboratories", "Has no relevance to nursing practice", "Is concerned solely with building abstract theoretical models"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 173, 'Observation in research involves:', '["Watching behaviour in its natural setting", "Interviewing participants", "Recording everything that happens", "Reviewing records only"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 174, 'Which term best describes the process of obtaining, deploying, and utilizing a variety of essential resources to contribute to an organization’s success?', '["planning", "organizing", "staffing", "management"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 175, 'Which best captures the organising function of a manager?', '["Putting work together", "Delegating duties", "Breaking down work into operating duties and positions", "All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 176, 'Which of the following is the best indicator of adequate staffing?', '["Number of beds", "Number of doctors", "Patient acuity and workload", "Hospital size"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 177, 'When evaluating whether a teaching/health education session was effective, the nurse should primarily assess:', '["Whether the session ran on schedule only", "Whether the learner''s knowledge, skills or behaviour actually changed as intended", "How many slides were used", "The room temperature during the session"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 178, 'Conflict arising from unclear or overlapping job expectations between two staff members is known as:', '["Intragroup conflict", "Role conflict", "Intrapersonal conflict", "Distributive conflict"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 179, 'The following are benefits of teamwork EXCEPT:', '["Reduced medical errors", "Increased patient safety", "Improved productivity", "Creating ego"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 180, 'In Herzberg''s theory, hygiene (maintenance) factors and motivators:', '["Hygiene factors are more important than motivators", "Motivators alone create motivation without hygiene factors", "The two operate entirely independently", "Work interdependently and complement each other"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 181, 'Which document outlines the duties and responsibilities of a staff nurse?', '["Ward report", "Duty roster", "Job description", "Nursing care plan"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 182, 'The nurse manager wants to assess the compliance of staff with a new hand hygiene protocol. Which type of audit would be most effective for this purpose?', '["Retrospective audit", "Concurrent audit", "Outcome audit", "Chart audit"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 183, 'The nurse manager''s role in infection prevention includes:', '["Ensuring compliance with infection prevention policies, surveillance, education, and appropriate use of PPE", "Delegating all responsibility to cleaners", "Focusing only on environmental cleaning", "Reporting infections only at year-end"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 184, 'A nurse overriding a hesitant colleague''s objection to start immediate CPR during a cardiac arrest is an example of which conflict style?', '["Avoiding", "Competing", "Accommodating", "Collaborating"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 185, 'A failure in professional duty, practice or skill that leads to injury or harm to the patient is termed as-', '["Malpractice", "Negligence", "Crime", "Misunderstanding"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 186, 'A patient with brain death is a potential organ donor. The nurse ensures:', '["Cessation of vasopressors", "Withdrawal of life support", "Maintenance of hemodynamic stability", "Family presence during testing"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 187, 'Euthanasia, in the context of medical ethics, refers to:', '["Standard end-of-life comfort/palliative care", "The deliberate act of ending a person''s life to relieve suffering", "A type of anaesthesia used in surgery", "Routine resuscitation"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 188, 'In order of superiority (highest first), the Nigerian courts can be arranged as:', '["Supreme Court, Court of Appeal, High Court, Magistrate Court, Area Court", "Area Court, Supreme Court, Court of Appeal, High Court, Magistrate Court", "Supreme Court, Area Court, High Court, Magistrate Court, Court of Appeal", "Magistrate Court, Supreme Court, Court of Appeal, High Court, Area Court"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 189, 'A patient with a terminal illness has uncontrolled pain. Palliative sedation is considered when:', '["Pain is refractory to standard treatments", "The family requests it", "Death is imminent", "The patient has depression"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 190, 'Handing over patient care at shift change (handover/handing-taking-over) is legally and clinically important primarily because:', '["It is purely a formality with no real clinical value", "Incomplete or inaccurate handover can lead to errors and gaps in continuity of care, with legal implications if harm results", "It replaces the need for any documentation", "It is only required once per week"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 191, 'A nurse receiving handover for a new patient is told the patient has a DNR (Do Not Resuscitate) order. If the patient develops a reversible airway obstruction (e.g. from a food bolus, unrelated to their terminal condition), the nurse should:', '["Do nothing since DNR means withholding all care", "Still intervene appropriately for a reversible emergency, since DNR specifically refers to withholding cardiopulmonary resuscitation, not all treatment", "Wait for family permission before any action", "Immediately begin chest compressions regardless of the obstruction"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 192, 'Damage in law means:', '["Injury sustained on the football pitch", "Loss of a wrist watch in the market", "Loss or injury to person or property", "Loss of money in an armed robbery"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 193, 'A nurse is asked by a patient''s employer for information about the patient''s diagnosis and prognosis. The nurse''s appropriate response is to:', '["Provide the requested information immediately, since the employer is asking on the patient''s behalf", "Decline to share any information without the patient''s explicit, documented consent, citing confidentiality obligations", "Share only positive information", "Share information only if the employer insists strongly"]'::jsonb, 1),
(7, 194, 'A patient’s living will specifies no feeding tubes. During a stroke, the nurse honors this by:', '["Inserting an NG tube", "Initiating TPN", "Providing oral comfort feeds", "Withholding all nutrition"]'::jsonb, 2),
(7, 195, 'A patient on TPN has hyperglycemia. The nurse should:', '["Add insulin to TPN", "Decrease dextrose concentration", "Administer subcutaneous insulin", "A or C"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 196, 'When can early feeding be initiated in critically-ill patients?', '["6-12 hours", "12-18 hours", "18-24 hours", "24-48 hours"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 197, 'In patients with Cystic Fibrosis, abnormally thick mucus leads to obstruction of the secretory ducts of the __ that can contribute to malnutrition and diabetes.', '["Spleen", "Bladder", "Liver", "Pancreas"]'::jsonb, 3),
(7, 198, 'During prolonged starvation (beyond several days), the body increasingly relies on which fuel source to spare protein and preserve lean body mass?', '["Ketone bodies derived from fatty acid oxidation", "Dietary fibre", "Increased glycogenolysis", "Increased dietary protein intake"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 199, 'Vibration as an airway clearance technique is contraindicated in:', '["Bronchospasm and wheezing", "Retained secretions", "Atelectasis", "Cystic fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 0),
(7, 200, 'How many bronchopulmonary segments does the right lung have?', '["6", "8", "10", "12"]'::jsonb, 2);
